# d3Sea — Muireann on the north headland
#
# FIRST CONTACT. She initiates: the player has just landed and she is
# already looking at them. Her oracular register is NOT used here — the
# warning poem about the man who is not himself belongs to a later visit,
# after the player has met someone it could apply to.
#
# Shape: she challenges -> greeting -> she asks the name -> whose shore ->
# Fionnbarra -> the toll-man and the password -> where art thou bound
# (truth / evasion, which forks her closing) -> the ráth.
#
# ⚠ TWO THINGS THIS FORMAT CANNOT DO YET
#
#   1. THE GREETING LADDER. compile.mjs knows nothing of ladder/pool/
#      enterLadder, and they are not in KEY_ORDER either. If d3Sea.js has a
#      hand-written ladder, COMPILING THIS WILL DELETE IT. Check first:
#          grep -n "ladder" public/data/bog/d3Sea.js
#      Node 0 below is written as plain options as a placeholder.
#
#   2. EASCA NAME INPUT. No directive exists for it. Marked [[ EASCA ]].
#
# ── FACTS OUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   met_muireann          she has spoken; used by Mór at the ráth
#   gave_name / withheld_name
#   knows_fionnbarra
#   has_druid_word        the toll-man password — Odhrán's fourth crossing
#   told_muireann_truth / evaded_muireann
#   knows_rath            the ráth is now a place the player knows of

@file d3Sea


= node 0 — the hail
Who art thou that rows so bold
towards the Boyne's wide, bitter mouth?

# PLACEHOLDER FOR THE GREETING LADDER. What should happen here is the
# stacking blessing — wind, tide, the herons of the fen — each rung
# repeating what came before and adding one, which is the form she is
# testing the player against. As two flat options it is only a sketch.

* Return her greeting
@note met_muireann
> Wind that carried me greet thee.
< Stone of the shore greet thee.

* Say nothing
@note met_muireann
> [[ the player says nothing, or something graceless ]]
< [[ she notes it. not offended — a druid is not offended by a stranger's ignorance — but something has been learned about him. ]]

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 1 — the name
[[ short. she is waiting. ]]

# The player chose this name three scenes ago and may well have forgotten
# it. Being asked to type it again, by someone who has a reason to ask, is
# worth more than a menu confirming it.
#
# Worth deciding: is refusing a real option? "My quarrel is with my own
# name, too small yet for any song" is the best line in your sketch and it
# is a refusal. The player IS the unnamed champion, so a game that lets you
# withhold your name from a druid on first meeting is saying something. But
# it costs you the Easca beat, so the two pull against each other.

* Give your name
@note gave_name
> [[ EASCA — the player types the name they chose. ]]
< [[ she repeats it back. once, plainly, and does not comment on it. ]]

* Refuse it
@note withheld_name
> No ill thing have I in mind. No man's kin, no man's kine.
> My quarrel is with my own name, too small yet for any song.
< [[ she lets it stand. she does not ask twice. ]]

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 2 — whose shore
[[ short, or nothing. ]]

* What chieftain's shore is this?
@note knows_fionnbarra
> What chieftain's shore is this?
< Thy little boat has reached the mouth of Bóinn the bright and winding.
< This shore belongs first to her.
< Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse is Chief of this grey headland
< and of the three valleys that run north.

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 3 — the chief
[[ short. ]]

* What kind of man is Fionnbarra?
> What kind of man is Chief Fionnbarra?
< Fionnbarra is not an unjust man.
< His hounds are many.
< But his toll-man waits at the river with a face like an angry stone.

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 4 — the toll-man
[[ short. ]]

# This is the load-bearing one. has_druid_word is the fourth way across
# Odhrán's ford, and the only one that costs him nothing to grant.

* His toll-man?
@note has_druid_word
> His toll-man?
< If Fionnbarra's toll-man should meet thee at the strand,
< thou art to say that Muireann the Druid knows thy name.

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 5 — where art thou bound
And where now does the curragh turn its nose?
What draws thee south along this shore?

* Tell her plainly
@note told_muireann_truth
> From the grey isle that lies where sky meets the cold sea-wind.
> Long the crossing, hard the oar.
> To the bog that drinks men whole, Allen's dark and sunken ground.
> There the Fianna test their own. There I mean to prove my worth.
< [[ she takes this in. no ceremony about it. ]]

* Tell her less
@note evaded_muireann
> Ask the heron of the fen. He will know my road before me.
< Speak plain. What business brings a stranger to these lands?
> I have come from where the hound lost the scent
> and the hawk lost the wind beneath her wing.
> From where the fire was, that is not now burning.
> The sea is a poor keeper of roads.
< So. So.
< Thou art careful in thy answer.

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 6 — she answers the plain answer
@when note told_muireann_truth
[[ short. ]]

* Fionn will teach me
> Fionn I have heard of. Fionn the great one.
> Fionn, they say, shall teach me more.
< Fionn keeps poetry before the sword.
< [[ said flatly, as a fact about Fionn rather than as advice — it should be possible to miss entirely. ]]

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 7 — she answers the careful answer
@when note evaded_muireann
Where then art thou bound?

* Say even less
> There is a thing that waits for finding, and it is not yet found.
> I cannot tell thee the name of the house before I have stood within it.
< Go then. Go where thou art not going.
< Arrive where thou art not bound.
< [[ she is amused rather than obstructed. this is a form she knows. ]]

* Row on
@exit
@silent


= node 8 — the road
Some purpose drives thee, I think.

# The ráth is reached by hospitality, not permission. A stranger on that
# road WILL be fed; not going is the strange act. Nobody forbids anything,
# and Odhrán is physically in the way regardless.

* Which way, then?
@note knows_rath
> Which way, then?
< Half a morning's walk northwest along the track. Thou canst not miss the ringfort.
< Thou wilt find a fire, a full bowl, and dry ground there.
> And after?
< That great dark place, with its waters the colour of old bronze.
< It is not far. Two days west, two days south. Keep the hills to thy left hand.
< Travel well.

* Slán agat
@exit
> [[ the parting. ]]
< Go not by night.
< And if thou hear the sound of pipes where no man stands,
< thou hast heard nothing. Walk on.
< May the Boyne remember thy name to the sea.
< May the sea speak thy name to the deep places.
